arm64: dts: rockchip: khadas-edge: rkvdec core and CABAC clocks at 400 MHz

Khadas Edge1, Android 14 on mainline 6.12. Applied to kernel/mainline by
build/build-kernel.sh before every build (device/khadas/edge/kernel/patches/).

rkvdec takes four clocks. The CRU node sets ACLK_VDU (400 MHz) among its
assigned clocks; nothing sets SCLK_VDU_CORE and SCLK_VDU_CA, the decoder's
core and CABAC engines, and rkvdec does not either - they run at whatever the
bootloader or the reset left in CLKSEL_CON9. Rockchip's own kernel runs them
at 297 MHz, and at 396 MHz for heavy streams (rockchip,advanced-rates, the
rate 4K needs). Card 26 decoded 1440p VP9 at about 9 frames per second.

Both at 400 MHz here: CPLL (800 MHz, assigned by the CRU node) divided by
two. The bootwatch's playing-N.txt records the clock tree while a video
plays, which says what the rates were before and are now.

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3399-khadas-edge.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3399-khadas-edge.dtsi
index 87da6d5f2..97d8accd5 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3399-khadas-edge.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3399-khadas-edge.dtsi
@@ -913,3 +913,14 @@ fusb1_int: fusb1-int {
 		};
 	};
 };
+
+/*
+ * rkvdec's core and CABAC clocks: nothing in mainline sets them (the CRU node
+ * assigns ACLK_VDU, 400 MHz, not these), so they run at whatever the bootloader
+ * or the reset left. Rockchip's kernel runs them at 297 MHz and at 396 MHz for
+ * heavy streams (rockchip,advanced-rates): 400 MHz here, CPLL / 2.
+ */
+&vdec {
+	assigned-clocks = <&cru SCLK_VDU_CA>, <&cru SCLK_VDU_CORE>;
+	assigned-clock-rates = <400000000>, <400000000>;
+};
